# Systems Architecture Standards

When designing system boundaries, schemas, or technical proposals:

# Universal Software Architecture Standards

These guidelines enforce modularity, loose coupling, strict interface boundaries, and high cohesion when designing or reviewing software architecture.

---

## 1. Single Responsibility & High Cohesion
- Every module, service, or class must have a single clear reason to change.
- Keep business logic decoupled from transport protocols (HTTP/gRPC/CLI) and storage mechanisms (SQL/NoSQL/Key-Value).

---

## 2. Dependency Inversion & Interface Segregation
- Depend upon abstractions, not concrete implementations.
- Keep interfaces minimal and focused on client requirements rather than exposing kitchen-sink API surfaces.

---

## 3. Explicit Data & Schema Contracts
- Define unambiguous data transfer schemas with validation rules before implementing service boundaries.
- Maintain backwards compatibility for external APIs and persisted schemas. Plan explicit migration pathways for schema changes.

---

## 4. Architectural Decision Records (ADRs)
- Document non-trivial architectural decisions with:
  - Context & Problem Statement
  - Considered Alternatives
  - Decision & Rationale
  - Trade-offs & Consequences
